<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglo-German Naval Agreement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1935</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anglo-German Naval Agreement, signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 18 June 1935, allowed Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to build a navy of up to 35 per cent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>'s surface tonnage and 45 per cent of its submarine tonnage. By accepting a breach of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> without consulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> weakened the common front against German rearmament. Hitler renounced the agreement in April 1939.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglo-german-naval-agreement.jpg" alt="Anglo-German Naval Agreement" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp;amp; Ewing, photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitler_speech_means_end_of_peace_talks,_Senator_Borah_says._Washington,_D.C.,_April_28._Senator_William_E._Borah,_Republican_of_Idaho,_Dean_of_the_Senate_Foreign_Relations_Committee,_after_LCCN2016875531_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
